  "title": "What a chain of contacts can carry",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Three rings in a chain, A and B joined by contact c1 and B and C by contact c2, written as one space-time law; a contact binds two rings at lag T when every minimally decisive set of its value meets both rings' own first-row cells. (1) With listening contacts, which read their rings and feed nothing back, c2 never depends on ring A and c1 never on ring C, so the chain never binds its ends. (2) With shared contacts, which both of their rings read, c2(T) does not depend on ring A's first row for T <= d, d the distance between the two contacts along ring B, and symmetrically for c1 and ring C; the ends can be bound only from lag d + 1. (3) With linear or affine rules and affine contact laws, a contact binds two rings at lag T exactly when the support of its row of M^T meets both.",
  "dependencies": [
